„­bible_version£KJV¤book§chapter¨sectionsš…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¨Samuel also said unto Saul, The LORD sent me to anoint thee to be king over his people, over Israel: now therefore hearken thou unto the voice of the words of the LORD.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙŠThus saith the LORD of hosts, I remember that which Amalek did to Israel, how he laid wait for him in the way, when he came up from Egypt.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ£Now go and smite Amalek, and utterly destroy all that they have, and spare them not; but slay both man and woman, infant and suckling, ox and sheep, camel and ass.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse	¦verses–„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ€And Saul gathered the people together, and numbered them in Telaim, two hundred thousand footmen, and ten thousand men of Judah.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ?And Saul came to a city of Amalek, and laid wait in the valley.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙúAnd Saul said unto the Kenites, Go, depart, get you down from among the Amalekites, lest I destroy you with them: for ye shewed kindness to all the children of Israel, when they came up out of Egypt. So the Kenites departed from among the Amalekites.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙaAnd Saul smote the Amalekites from Havilah until thou comest to Shur, that is over against Egypt.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙsAnd he took Agag the king of the Amalekites alive, and utterly destroyed all the people with the edge of the sword.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse	¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙöBut Saul and the people spared Agag, and the best of the sheep, and of the oxen, and of the fatlings, and the lambs, and all that was good, and would not utterly destroy them: but every thing that was vile and refuse, that they destroyed utterly.¦layout ªlast_verse	…¤book§chapter«first_verse
