¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ3Then came the word of the LORD unto Samuel, saying,¦layout ªlast_verse
„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÁIt repenteth me that I have set up Saul to be king: for he is turned back from following me, and hath not performed my commandments. And it grieved Samuel; and he cried unto the LORD all night.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÇAnd when Samuel rose early to meet Saul in the morning, it was told Samuel, saying, Saul came to Carmel, and, behold, he set him up a place, and is gone about, and passed on, and gone down to Gilgal.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ{And Samuel came to Saul: and Saul said unto him, Blessed be thou of the LORD: I have performed the commandment of the LORD.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙtAnd Samuel said, What meaneth then this bleating of the sheep in mine ears, and the lowing of the oxen which I hear?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÃAnd Saul said, They have brought them from the Amalekites: for the people spared the best of the sheep and of the oxen, to sacrifice unto the LORD thy God; and the rest we have utterly destroyed.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ~Then Samuel said unto Saul, Stay, and I will tell thee what the LORD hath said to me this night. And he said unto him, Say on.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙœAnd Samuel said, When thou wast little in thine own sight, wast thou not made the head of the tribes of Israel, and the LORD anointed thee king over Israel?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙAnd the LORD sent thee on a journey, and said, Go and utterly destroy the sinners the Amalekites, and fight against them until they be consumed.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ€Wherefore then didst thou not obey the voice of the LORD, but didst fly upon the spoil, and didst evil in the sight of the LORD?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÇAnd Saul said unto Samuel, Yea, I have obeyed the voice of the LORD, and have gone the way which the LORD sent me, and have brought Agag the king of Amalek, and have utterly destroyed the Amalekites.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¡But the people took of the spoil, sheep and oxen, the chief of the things which should have been utterly destroyed, to sacrifice unto the LORD thy God in Gilgal.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses’„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines•‚¤text°And Samuel said,¦layout ‚¤textÙAHath the LORD as great delight in burnt offerings and sacrifices,¦layout§quote_1‚¤textÙ$As in obeying the voice of the LORD?¦layout§quote_1‚¤textÙ)Behold, to obey is better than sacrifice,¦layout§quote_1‚¤textÙ$And to hearken than the fat of rams.¦layout§quote_1ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines”‚¤textÙ*For rebellion is as the sin of witchcraft,¦layout ‚¤textÙ-And stubbornness is as iniquity and idolatry.¦layout§quote_1‚¤textÙ0Because thou hast rejected the word of the LORD,¦layout§quote_1‚¤textÙ+He hath also rejected thee from being king.¦layout§quote_1ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses–„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¢And Saul said unto Samuel, I have sinned: for I have transgressed the commandment of the LORD, and thy words: because I feared the people, and obeyed their voice.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ_Now therefore, I pray thee, pardon my sin, and turn again with me, that I may worship the LORD.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¡And Samuel said unto Saul, I will not return with thee: for thou hast rejected the word of the LORD, and the LORD hath rejected thee from being king over Israel.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ^And as Samuel turned about to go away, he laid hold upon the skirt of his mantle, and it rent.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ›And Samuel said unto him, The LORD hath rent the kingdom of Israel from thee this day, and hath given it to a neighbour of thine, that is better than thou.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙdAnd also the Strength of Israel will not lie nor repent: for he is not a man, that he should repent.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses’„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¬Then he said, I have sinned: yet honour me now, I pray thee, before the elders of my people, and before Israel, and turn again with me, that I may worship the LORD thy God.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ@So Samuel turned again after Saul; and Saul worshipped the LORD.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verse ªlast_verse!¦verses’„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¢Then said Samuel, Bring ye hither to me Agag the king of the Amalekites. And Agag came unto him delicately. And Agag said, Surely the bitterness of death is past.¦layout ªlast_verse „¥verse!¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¡And Samuel said, As thy sword hath made women childless, so shall thy mother be childless among women. And Samuel hewed Agag in pieces before the LORD in Gilgal.¦layout ªlast_verse!…¤book§chapter«first_verse"ªlast_verse#¦verses’„¥verse"¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙKThen Samuel went to Ramah; and Saul went up to his house to Gibeah of Saul.¦layout ªlast_verse"„¥verse#¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ£And Samuel came no more to see Saul until the day of his death: nevertheless Samuel mourned for Saul: and the LORD repented that he had made Saul king over Israel.¦layout ªlast_verse#